---
title: Firewall
order: 4
description: iptables, ip6tables, nftables, basic chains and rules
tags: [firewall, iptables, nftables, ip6tables, netfilter]
---

Linux firewall tools. nftables is the modern successor to iptables. Both
use the Netfilter framework in the kernel. On Artix, neither runs by
default; you must enable them via OpenRC services.

@code bash
# ---- iptables: traditional packet filtering ----

# list rules
iptables -L                               # list all chains
iptables -L -n -v                         # numeric, verbose
iptables -L -n --line-numbers             # with rule numbers
iptables -t nat -L -n -v                  # NAT table
iptables -t mangle -L -n -v              # mangle table
iptables -S                               # dump rules as commands

@code bash
# default policies
iptables -P INPUT DROP                    # drop incoming by default
iptables -P FORWARD DROP                  # drop forwarded by default
iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT                 # allow outgoing by default

@code bash
# basic rules
# allow loopback
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT

# allow established/related connections
iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# allow SSH
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# allow HTTP/HTTPS
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT

# allow ping
iptables -A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT

# allow from specific subnet
iptables -A INPUT -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j ACCEPT

# drop and log
iptables -A INPUT -j LOG --log-prefix "iptables-drop: "
iptables -A INPUT -j DROP

@code bash
# rule management
iptables -D INPUT 3                       # delete rule by number
iptables -D INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT   # delete by spec
iptables -I INPUT 1 -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT # insert at position
iptables -R INPUT 1 -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT # replace rule 1
iptables -F                               # flush all rules
iptables -X                               # delete user-defined chains
iptables -Z                               # zero packet counters

@code bash
# NAT / port forwarding
# enable IP forwarding first
echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward
# or persistently in /etc/sysctl.d/99-sysctl.conf:
# net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1

# masquerade (typical for NAT gateway)
iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE

# port forward: external:8080 -> internal:80
iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 8080 -j DNAT --to 192.168.1.10:80
iptables -A FORWARD -p tcp -d 192.168.1.10 --dport 80 -j ACCEPT

@code bash
# save and restore
iptables-save > /etc/iptables/iptables.rules
iptables-restore < /etc/iptables/iptables.rules

# ip6tables works identically for IPv6
ip6tables -L -n -v
ip6tables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
ip6tables-save > /etc/iptables/ip6tables.rules

# OpenRC service
rc-service iptables save
rc-service iptables start
rc-update add iptables default

@text
nftables is the replacement for iptables. It has a cleaner syntax, better
performance, and combines IPv4/IPv6 in a single framework.

@code bash
# ---- nftables ----

# list everything
nft list ruleset                          # full ruleset
nft list tables                           # list tables
nft list table inet filter                # specific table
nft list chain inet filter input          # specific chain

@code bash
# create a basic firewall
nft add table inet filter
nft add chain inet filter input '{ type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop; }'
nft add chain inet filter forward '{ type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop; }'
nft add chain inet filter output '{ type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept; }'

# add rules
nft add rule inet filter input iif lo accept
nft add rule inet filter input ct state established,related accept
nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport 22 accept
nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport {80, 443} accept
nft add rule inet filter input ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 accept
nft add rule inet filter input icmp type echo-request accept
nft add rule inet filter input counter drop

@code bash
# nftables config file approach (preferred)
# /etc/nftables.conf

#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset

table inet filter {
    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
        iif lo accept
        ct state established,related accept
        tcp dport 22 accept
        tcp dport { 80, 443 } accept
        ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 accept
        icmp type echo-request accept
        counter drop
    }
    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
    }
    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
    }
}

@code bash
# nftables NAT
nft add table inet nat
nft add chain inet nat postrouting '{ type nat hook postrouting priority 100; }'
nft add chain inet nat prerouting '{ type nat hook prerouting priority -100; }'
nft add rule inet nat postrouting oifname "eth0" masquerade
nft add rule inet nat prerouting iifname "eth0" tcp dport 8080 dnat to 192.168.1.10:80

@code bash
# rule management
nft delete rule inet filter input handle 7    # delete by handle
nft -a list chain inet filter input           # show handles
nft insert rule inet filter input position 3 tcp dport 22 accept
nft flush chain inet filter input             # flush chain
nft flush ruleset                             # flush everything
nft delete table inet filter                  # delete table

# save and load
nft list ruleset > /etc/nftables.conf
nft -f /etc/nftables.conf                     # load from file

# OpenRC service
rc-service nftables start
rc-update add nftables default

@text
Do not run iptables and nftables simultaneously. They share the same
Netfilter hooks and will conflict. Choose one.

iptables-nft is a compatibility layer that translates iptables commands
to nftables rules. On Artix, the iptables package may be iptables-nft
by default. Check with: `iptables --version` (look for "nf_tables").
